---

<BaseLayout
  title="Altar of the Heart: An Undivided Heart for God | DMI Calamba City"
  description="A Bible study from Psalm 86:11 about undivided allegiance, daily devotion, repentance, prayer, and a life fully offered to God."
  bodyClass="study-page"
>
  <header class="study-hero">
    <nav class="study-nav" aria-label="Primary navigation" data-i18n-aria-label="navAria">
      <a class="brand-lockup" href={base} aria-label="Return to DMI Calamba City home" data-i18n-aria-label="homeAria">
        <img src={logoPath} width="900" height="900" alt="" />
        <span>DMI · Calamba City</span>
      </a>
      <LanguageToggle translations={translations} />
      <span>02 August 2026</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="study-hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="eyebrow">Bible Study · Psalm 86:11</p>
      <h1 data-i18n="title">Altar of the Heart</h1>
      <p class="study-subtitle" data-i18n="subtitle">An Undivided Heart for God</p>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="hero-scripture">
      <p lang="en" data-i18n="heroScripture" data-i18n-lang>“Teach me your way, O LORD, that I may walk in your truth; unite my heart to fear your name.”</p>
      <cite lang="en" data-i18n="heroCitation" data-i18n-lang>Psalm 86:11 ESV</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </header>

  <main class="study-content">
    <aside class="study-aside" aria-label="Study summary" data-i18n-aria-label="summaryAria">
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="themeLabel">Theme</span>
        <p data-i18n="theme">Unite the heart. Clear the altar. Tend the fire.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="keyTextsLabel">Key texts</span>
        <p data-i18n="keyTexts">Psalm 86:10–11<br />Ezekiel 14:3–6<br />Leviticus 6:12–13<br />1 Thessalonians 5:16–19<br />Romans 12:1</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="timeLabel">Time</span>
        <p data-i18n="time">25–30 minutes</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="presentationLabel">Presentation</span>
        <a class="aside-action" href="https://www.canva.com/d/MG3GYTQD8aNbpV7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="presentationLink">Open Canva slides <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="aside-label" data-i18n="serviceLabel">Sunday service</span>
        <a class="aside-action" href="https://www.youtube.com/live/SkIZpXs_e_o?si=p-eZGkAaVs4RSLol" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" data-i18n="serviceLink">Watch the preaching <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <article class="study-article">
      <section class="opening" aria-labelledby="opening-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section01">01 · Begin here</p>
        <h2 id="opening-title" data-i18n="openingTitle">When the altar is crowded</h2>
        <p class="lead" data-i18n="openingLead">
          Most believers do not wake up intending to abandon God. The subtler danger is continuing to worship him while other loyalties grow beside him. The problem is not always a cold heart; sometimes <strong>the altar is crowded</strong>.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="openingBody">
          We want God, but also control. We want his will, but also approval. David does not merely ask for stronger feelings; he asks God to make his inner life one. An idol is anything created that gains veto power over our obedience and receives the ultimate trust, fear, allegiance, or love belonging to God.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement">
          <span data-i18n="keyTruthLabel">Key truth</span>
          <p data-i18n="keyTruth">The greatest threat to sustained devotion is not always opposition around us. It is divided allegiance within us.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-one">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section02">02 · Receive</p>
        <h2 id="movement-one" data-i18n="movementOneTitle">Let God unite the heart</h2>
        <p data-i18n="movementOneBody1">
          Psalm 86:11 moves in a clear order: <strong>teach me</strong>, so that I may <strong>walk</strong>, and <strong>unite my heart</strong>. David begins with God’s way, not a path he has already selected. An undivided heart prays, “Teach me, even when your way confronts mine.” Truth is not simply information to admire; it becomes a life of obedience.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="psalmVerse" data-i18n-lang>“For you are great and do wondrous things; you alone are God.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="psalmCitation" data-i18n-lang>Psalm 86:10</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="movementOneBody2">
          “Unite my heart” is a prayer for an integrated inner life—thought, will, affection, and intention gathered around reverent allegiance to God. Because God alone is God, no competing master deserves the throne. A divided heart reserves one room; an undivided heart welcomes Jesus as Lord of every room.
        </p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss1Label">Discuss · 1</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss1">Where is my heart divided between God’s way and another way?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-two">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section03">03 · Release</p>
        <h2 id="movement-two" data-i18n="movementTwoTitle">Clear the altar of rival loves</h2>
        <p data-i18n="movementTwoBody1">
          In Ezekiel 14, the elders sit before God’s prophet while carrying idols within. Their posture looks spiritual, but their allegiance is already decided. They want guidance without surrender.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerses">Key verses · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="ezekielVerse3" data-i18n-lang>“Son of man, these men have taken their idols into their hearts, and set the stumbling block of their iniquity before their faces.”</p>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="ezekielVerse6" data-i18n-lang>“Thus says the Lord GOD: Repent and turn away from your idols, and turn away your faces from all your abominations.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="ezekielCitation" data-i18n-lang>Ezekiel 14:3, 6</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="movementTwoBody2">
          Hidden idols often become visible when threatened—through controlling fear, disproportionate anger, deep defensiveness, secret compromise, or refusal to obey. God’s invitation is direct: <strong>repent and turn away</strong>. Repentance is not self-hatred; it is the end of defending what God is confronting.
        </p>
        <ul class="diagnostic-list" aria-label="Questions for recognizing an idol" data-i18n-aria-label="idolQuestionsAria">
          <li data-i18n="idolQuestion1">What do I fear losing most?</li>
          <li data-i18n="idolQuestion2">Which outcome am I demanding from God?</li>
          <li data-i18n="idolQuestion3">Whose approval controls my obedience?</li>
        </ul>
        <p data-i18n="movementTwoBody3">
          Through Christ, repentance is not an attempt to persuade a reluctant God to receive us. Grace has already opened the way home. God exposes rival loves not merely to shame us, but to reclaim our hearts.
        </p>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss2Label">Discuss · 2</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss2">Which reaction may be revealing a rival love—and what am I asking God to bless that he may be asking me to surrender?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-three">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section04">04 · Practice</p>
        <h2 id="movement-three" data-i18n="movementThreeTitle">Tend the altar daily</h2>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerses">Key verses · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="leviticusVerses" data-i18n-lang>“The fire on the altar shall be kept burning on it; it shall not go out. The priest shall burn wood on it every morning, and he shall arrange the burnt offering on it and shall burn on it the fat of the peace offerings. Fire shall be kept burning on the altar continually; it shall not go out.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="leviticusCitation" data-i18n-lang>Leviticus 6:12–13</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="movementThreeBody1">
          This command originally concerned Israel’s physical altar of burnt offering. The priests removed ashes, added fresh wood, arranged the sacrifice, and guarded the flame. The picture teaches a daily rhythm: <strong>yesterday’s fire cannot replace today’s obedience</strong>. Past experiences with God are testimonies, not fuel reserves.
        </p>
        <div class="practice-grid" aria-label="Daily practices for tending devotion" data-i18n-aria-label="practicesAria">
          <div>
            <span data-i18n="removeAshes">Remove the ashes</span>
            <p data-i18n="removeAshesBody">Unconfessed sin, bitterness, secret compromise, pride, destructive habits, and spiritual apathy.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span data-i18n="addWood">Add fresh wood</span>
            <p data-i18n="addWoodBody">Scripture, prayer, worship, obedience, fellowship, confession, fasting, service, and thanksgiving.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p data-i18n="movementThreeBody2">
          We cannot manufacture the Holy Spirit or earn God’s presence through religious effort. God supplies the flame; discipleship tends the altar. Grace does not produce passivity—it teaches us to remain attentive and responsive.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerses">Key verses · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="thessaloniansVerses" data-i18n-lang>“Rejoice always, pray without ceasing, give thanks in all circumstances; for this is the will of God in Christ Jesus for you. Do not quench the Spirit.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="thessaloniansCitation" data-i18n-lang>1 Thessalonians 5:16–19</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="movementThreeBody3">
          Unceasing prayer is not nonstop speech. It is a life that keeps returning anxieties, decisions, conviction, thanksgiving, failure, and surrender to God. Prayer is the rhythm by which the scattered heart repeatedly returns to its center.
        </p>
        <div class="key-statement dark">
          <span data-i18n="rememberLabel">Remember</span>
          <p data-i18n="remember">God gives the flame. We remain teachable, remove what quenches his work, add what keeps us attentive, and respond in obedient faith.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="discussion-prompt">
          <span data-i18n="discuss3Label">Discuss · 3</span>
          <p data-i18n="discuss3">What needs to be removed, and what one daily practice needs to be added, so my heart remains responsive to God?</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="movement-four">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section05">05 · Offer</p>
        <h2 id="movement-four" data-i18n="movementFourTitle">Place the whole life before God</h2>
        <blockquote class="verse-card">
          <span data-i18n="keyVerse">Key verse · ESV</span>
          <p lang="en" data-i18n="romansVerse" data-i18n-lang>“I appeal to you therefore, brothers, by the mercies of God, to present your bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable to God, which is your spiritual worship.”</p>
          <cite lang="en" data-i18n="romansCitation" data-i18n-lang>Romans 12:1</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p data-i18n="movementFourBody1">
          “The altar of the heart” is a biblical picture, not a phrase quoted directly from Scripture. Romans 12 gives the response behind the picture: the offering God desires is not one isolated religious moment, but the whole life—body, thoughts, ambitions, relationships, finances, private habits, public service, and future.
        </p>
        <p data-i18n="movementFourBody2">
          Because a living sacrifice can step away, surrender must be renewed. Daily we say, “Lord, everything belongs to you again.” The goal is not temporary spiritual excitement, but sustained covenant faithfulness: a heart wholly available to God.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="response-section" aria-labelledby="response-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="section06">06 · A simple response</p>
        <h2 id="response-title" data-i18n="responseTitle">Unite. Clear. Tend.</h2>
        <ol class="response-steps">
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="uniteLabel">Unite</span>
            <p data-i18n="uniteBody">Pray Psalm 86:11 and invite God to gather every divided desire under the lordship of Jesus.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="clearLabel">Clear</span>
            <p data-i18n="clearBody">Name and renounce the rival love, resentment, compromise, or fear occupying the altar.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span data-i18n="tendLabel">Tend</span>
            <p data-i18n="tendBody">Offer your whole life again and choose one rhythm: Scripture before your phone, a midday prayer pause, or an evening heart review.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="closing-prayer" aria-labelledby="prayer-title">
        <p class="section-number" data-i18n="prayTogether">Pray together</p>
        <h2 id="prayer-title" data-i18n="prayerTitle">A prayer for an undivided heart</h2>
        <p data-i18n="prayer">
          Father, teach us your way and cause us to walk in your truth. Gather every scattered affection, ambition, fear, and desire. Expose the idols we carry into your presence, and free us from pride and self-defense. By your Spirit, teach us to repent without delay, pray without ceasing, and offer our whole lives to you. Jesus, you alone are God. Unite our hearts to fear your name. Amen.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="takeaway" aria-label="Study takeaway" data-i18n-aria-label="takeawayAria">
        <span data-i18n="takeawayLabel">Take this with you</span>
        <p data-i18n="takeaway">Unite the heart. Clear the altar. Tend the fire. Offer the whole life to God again today.</p>
      </section>

